# Method of Procedure (MOP)

## Kubernetes Monitoring & Alerting using Prometheus and Grafana

---

# 1. Objective

This document provides a **step-by-step procedure** to configure **Kubernetes monitoring and alerting** using **Prometheus and Grafana**, including:

* Accessing Grafana and Prometheus
* Creating Grafana dashboards
* Monitoring Pod CPU, Memory, Network, and Storage metrics
* Detecting Pod restarts
* Configuring alert rules
* Sending alert notifications via **SMTP email**

The procedure assumes:

* Prometheus and Grafana are already installed in the Kubernetes cluster
* User has **kubectl access**
* Grafana Admin access is available

---

# 2. Environment Prerequisites

Ensure the following access and tools are available.

### 2.1 Kubernetes Access

You must have access to the cluster.

Verify using:

```bash
kubectl get nodes
```

Check namespaces:

```bash
kubectl get ns
```

---

### 2.2 Monitoring Namespace

Most monitoring stacks run in:

```
monitoring namespace
```

Verify monitoring components:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n monitoring
```

Example output:

```
prometheus-grafana-0
prometheus-server
prometheus-alertmanager
```

---

### 2.3 Verify Prometheus Metrics

Open Prometheus UI:

```
http://<prometheus-url>
```

Go to:

```
Status → Targets
```

Ensure targets are **UP**.

Test metrics in Prometheus query console:

```
kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total
```

If metrics appear → Prometheus is working.

---

# 3. Accessing Grafana

Open Grafana UI:

```
http://<grafana-url>
```

Login using:

```
Admin credentials
```

Verify Prometheus datasource:

```
Connections → Data Sources → Prometheus
```

Datasource should be **Healthy**.

---

# 4. Creating a Custom Grafana Dashboard

## Step 1 — Create Dashboard

In Grafana:

```
Dashboards → New → New Dashboard
```

Click:

```
Add Visualization
```

Select datasource:

```
Prometheus
```

---

# 5. Pod Resource Monitoring Panels

## 5.1 CPU Usage by Pod

Add query:

```promql
sum(rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{container!="POD"}[2m])) by (namespace,pod)
```

Set visualization:

```
Time Series
```

Title:

```
Pod CPU Usage
```

---

## 5.2 Memory Usage by Pod

Query:

```promql
sum(container_memory_usage_bytes{container!="POD"}) by (namespace,pod)
```

Title:

```
Pod Memory Usage
```

---

## 5.3 Network Traffic

Query:

```promql
sum(rate(container_network_receive_bytes_total[2m])) by (namespace,pod)
```

Title:

```
Pod Network Receive Traffic
```

For transmit traffic:

```promql
sum(rate(container_network_transmit_bytes_total[2m])) by (namespace,pod)
```

---

## 5.4 Node Storage Usage

Query:

```promql
(node_filesystem_size_bytes - node_filesystem_free_bytes) 
/ node_filesystem_size_bytes
```

Title:

```
Node Disk Usage %
```

---

## 5.5 Pod Restart Counter

Query:

```promql
kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total
```

To filter namespaces:

```promql
sum by(namespace,pod)(
increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total{
namespace=~"namespace1|namespace2"
}[20m]))
```

Title:

```
Pod Restart Counter
```

---

# 6. Saving Dashboard

Click:

```
Save Dashboard
```

Provide name:

```
Kubernetes Pod Monitoring
```

